<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Dillingen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1551">1551</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of Dillingen was founded in 1551 by Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-truchsess-von-waldburg">Otto Truchsess von Waldburg</a>, prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in his residence town of Dillingen on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Run by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> from 1563, it trained Catholic clergy for southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and became a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. It was reduced to a lyceum in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-dillingen.jpg" alt="University of Dillingen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JohannesSch. (Serafim 89) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collegium_St_Hieronymi_Haupthaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-truchsess-von-waldburg">Otto Truchsess von Waldburg</a>, Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> from 1543 to 1573, first set up the College of St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a>, endowed with the revenues of several monasteries suppressed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, to educate the clergy and protect the Catholic faith. Pope Julius III raised it to a university in 1551, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> ratified its privileges, and it was formally inaugurated on 21 May 1554. Early professors came from Louvain University, among them the first rector, Peter Endavianus, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, among them Pedro de Soto.</p><p>In 1563 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> handed the teaching to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, whose provincial was then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-canisius">Peter Canisius</a>. The cathedral chapter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> disputed the transfer until Bishop Henry von Knöringen settled it on 14 June 1606, which earned him the name of second founder, and Emperor Ferdinand III ratified the new charter in 1641.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> taught the humanities, in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> that was then part of the university, philosophy and theology. A faculty of law with one professor of canon and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> was added early in the 17th century, and church history in 1738. Between 1564 and 1770 the university conferred the bachelor of arts on 7704 students and the master of arts on 5997. A seminary founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a> in 1585 educated more than 4,000 priests before it closed in 1798, and students came from all parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Christopher Scheiner, professor of mathematics, invented the pantograph.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the Swedish invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a> the university's revenues failed, professors were imprisoned, students scattered and lectures stopped, but it recovered after the peace and gained a lecture building in 1688. When the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was suppressed in 1773, the teaching of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> ended and Prince-Bishop Clement Wenceslaus placed the university directly under the bishop.</p><p>A new charter of 1786 brought deans to the faculties and lectures partly in German, but the loss of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college's lands left the university short of money, and by 1798–1799 it had only 109 students. In 1802 it passed with the cathedral chapter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, whose elector abolished it on 3 November 1803, founding a classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> and a lyceum for philosophy and theology in its place.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Dillingen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cardinal Otto Truchsess von Waldburg founded the <b>University of Dillingen</b> in 1551, run by the Jesuits from 1563, which made the territory a Counter-Reformation stronghold.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-augsburg">Prince-Bishopric of Augsburg</a></li></ul></section>
